sec.p:
We identify a marine heatwave (MHW) event as an anomalously warm SST event, whose value exceeds the 90th percentile of the daily SST distribution during a reference climatology period and persists for at least five consecutive days20. The 90th percentile threshold on a day is calculated using an 11-day window centered and is then smoothed using a 31-day moving window36. The MHW metrics (mean intensity, duration, frequency, and cumulative intensity) were calculated using OISST, ERA5, and CESM2(WACCM6) historical data for the period 1982–2014, which served as the climatological baseline for CESM2(WACCM6) validation. These metrics were also calculated for each ensemble member of the SSP2-4.5 and ARISE-SAI-1.5 simulations to represent the present-day (2015–2034) and future climates, using the present-day period as the climatological baseline. The metrics of the 10 ensembles were then averaged to obtain an ensemble mean for each metric. We also considered all SST anomalies associated with MHW events that exceeded 0.5 °C and arbitrarily set a threshold of 95% spatial coverage over the AB region. Based on the studies by Roy, et al.47, we assume that persistent SST anomalies greater than 0.5 °C will inevitably affect the marine ecosystem and fisheries of the entire AB if they cover at least 95% of the AB region. This approach allowed us to explore potential relationships between potentially harmful SST anomalies in the AB region and those in the broader study area. For the future projection of MHW metrics, we defined a rate of change for each metric, indicating the percentage increase or decrease at each grid point. This was calculated using the following formula:1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
